# Explore the Hidden Music of Cyclic Networks

A cyclic Cayley graph begins with modular arithmetic: vertices are residues in $\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z$, and a connection set $S$ tells us which translated neighbors can be reached. The remarkable fact is that the graph's natural vibrations are exactly the [discrete Fourier modes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discrete_Fourier_transform).

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The cycle widget displays the exact law $\lambda_k=2\cos(2\pi k/n)$. Use it first to build intuition, then inspect a static rendering.

{{visualization:1}}

## From jumps to eigenvalues

Every allowed displacement contributes a unit complex number. Their sum is the eigenvalue at that frequency:

$$\lambda_k=\sum_{s\in S}e^{2\pi i ks/n}.$$

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the diagonalization argument</summary>

For $\chi_k(x)=e^{2\pi ikx/n}$, the character identity gives $\chi_k(x+s)=\chi_k(x)\chi_k(s)$. Therefore

$$
(A_S\chi_k)(x)=\sum_{s\in S}\chi_k(x+s)
=\chi_k(x)\sum_{s\in S}\chi_k(s)
=\lambda_k\chi_k(x).
$$

The $n$ characters are an orthogonal basis, so this determines the complete spectrum.
</details>

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:2}}

## Symmetry forces reality

When every $s$ is paired with $-s$, conjugate phasors cancel their imaginary parts. This is the finite harmonic-analysis form of Hermitian self-adjointness.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the spectral bounds</summary>

At the trivial frequency every phasor equals $1$, so $\lambda_0=|S|$. At every frequency, the triangle inequality gives $|\lambda_k|\leq |S|$. If $S=-S$, then conjugation followed by the reindexing $s\mapsto -s$ proves $\overline{\lambda_k}=\lambda_k$.
</details>

## Compute at scale

The direct method is ideal for sparse connection sets. Dense sets benefit from the [fast Fourier transform](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fast_Fourier_transform), which computes the full spectrum in $O(n\log n)$ time.

