<details><summary>Click to reveal the mixing-time caveat</summary>

For a normalized classical operator, spectral separation controls decay of nonconstant modes under appropriate irreducibility and aperiodicity conditions. A genuinely quantum walk is unitary and requires a separate model and notion of mixing. The adjacency spectrum is indispensable input, but it does not by itself establish a universal quantum speedup. For background, compare [Cayley graphs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cayley_graph), [spectral graph theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spectral_graph_theory), and [quantum walks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_walk).
</details>
